# ATTENTION-TERM amendment (Chief, 2026-09-08): three wording edits on top of the Deputy's ded97f6 before landing

Applied in `~/worktrees/attention-term` on the delivered diff, tested there, landed with it as one
commit. The Deputy's patch is `ATTENTION-TERM.patch` beside this file and is otherwise unchanged.
None of the three changes a number the gate computes; each changes what the code says about a
number, where the record's arithmetic did not support the wording. The arithmetic is in the
review note appended to `research/records/ATTENTION-TERM-2026-09-08/README.md` in the same commit.

1. **The fit form.** The comment said 102.3 was "fitted on the two largest rows where the linear
   terms have washed out". It is the slope against T² between the 4,096 and 8,192 rows with a
   constant free. The per-length coefficient's fall (155 → 103.3) is a linear term over T², and a
   fit that allows one gives 100.5 B/token² with about 21 KiB per token linear, on all five rows
   and on the two largest alike. So 102.3 carries the linear remainder and errs high by under two
   percent. The comment now says the fit form and the direction; the gate keeps 102.3, because it
   is a bound in the right direction and the affine residual re-fit makes the envelope exact at
   every calibration row whichever coefficient is used.

2. **The forward finding.** "At 64 and 128 the forward peak equals the inputs to the byte" is a
   cancellation in the probe, not an empty kernel: the float32 sources of the random draws were
   still resident when the baseline was read (three times the bfloat16 inputs), and their release
   during the forward exactly offsets the output and the float32 cast the loss takes. The
   conclusion is unchanged and stronger than the wording, since an O(T) release cannot mask an
   O(T²) allocation. The comment now says "adds no term quadratic in the row" at 64 and 128 and
   "about 1.1 score matrices" at 256.

3. **The block count without checkpointing.** `blocks × 102.3` is an upper bound, not a
   measurement: the one-block figure includes the backward's transient part, which runs one block
   at a time and does not stack, while the forward-retained scores do. The docstring and the
   departure line now say so, and name the two-block measurement that would tighten it. The
   Deputy's reading of the scaling rule is upheld: a measured per-block cost times a counted
   number of blocks is not scaling an envelope by a variable it never saw, provided the product is
   declared a bound, which it now is. Pinning one block always would err low, the wrong direction.

Verification in the worktree: full suite 1,707 passed, 14 skipped, exit 0 on the delivered diff;
`tests/test_preflight.py` 124 passed on the delivered diff and again on the amended bytes; ruff
0 findings on both files, format drift unchanged from the delivered state.

